course: dictionary
lesson: 15373
title: Tools and idioms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A spanner is a tool. It is made of metal, and you use it to turn nuts and bolts so that they get tight or loose. Think of it as a tool for fixing things, like a bike wheel or a wobbly chair.
explain: «Dad used a spanner to tighten the bolt on my bike.» The clues are bike, bolt and tighten. Something that tightens a bolt must be a tool, so a spanner is a tool for turning. Ask yourself: what would fit here? A tool, not a fruit or a toy.
explain: Some phrases use a word in a different way. «Our trip was going well until the bus broke down and threw a spanner in the works.» No one threw a real tool. The bus breaking down spoiled the plan, so the phrase means to spoil a plan.
fact: A spanner is a metal tool for turning nuts and bolts.
fact: «Spanner» is a noun. One spanner, two spanners.
fact: «Tighten» means make tight, and «loosen» means make loose.
fact: «Throw a spanner in the works» is an idiom meaning to spoil a plan.
fact: When you meet a word in a phrase, check whether the sentence is about a real object or about something going wrong.
exercise: «Dad used a spanner to tighten the bolt on my bike.» What is a spanner? | *A metal tool for turning nuts and bolts | A type of bicycle seat | A soft toy for a baby
exercise: «The mechanic picked up a spanner and loosened the nut.» Find and copy one word that means make loose. | picked | loosened | mechanic | nut | *loosened and nut are both about the nut, so the answer is nut
exercise: «Our trip was going well until the bus broke down and threw a spanner in the works.» What does throw a spanner in the works mean? | Help the team finish early | *Spoil a plan | Fix the bus with a tool | Build something new
test: What is the plural of spanner? | spanneres | *spanners | spannies | spanner
test: Mia says a spanner is a kind of fruit. Is she right? | *No, a spanner is a metal tool | Yes, it is a vegetable | Yes, it grows on trees | No, it is a toy
exam: Which sentence uses spanner in an idiom? | «Pass me the spanner from the box.» | «The spanner was heavy and cold.» | *«The storm threw a spanner in the works of our sports day.» | «She used a spanner to fix the chair.»
